---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LiteralList from '../components/LiteralList.astro';
import YearTable from '../components/YearTable.astro';
import TiedRules from '../components/TiedRules.astro';
import ruleJson from '../data/rule.json';
import frontJson from '../data/frontier.json';
import metaJson from '../data/meta.json';
import type { Rule, Frontier, Meta } from '../data/types';
import { fmtPct, fmtDeg } from '../lib/labels';

const rule = ruleJson as unknown as Rule;
const front = frontJson as unknown as Frontier;
const meta = metaJson as unknown as Meta;
const sel = rule.selection;
const v = rule.verification;
const f1 = front.frontier.ec_T1;
const pvWrong = rule.popvote.wrong_years.join(' and ');
const t = front.targets;
const noise = meta.headline.p_perfect_rule_under_noise.T1;
const poolB = meta.search_size.pool_B_distinct_vectors.toLocaleString('en-US');
const noGameYears = rule.years.filter((y) => y.cells.some((c) => c.kind === 'event' && !c.held)).length;
const astroHours = (y: Rule['years'][number]) => Math.min(...y.cells.flatMap((c) => (c.kind === 'astro' ? [c.margin_hours] : [])));
const tightest = rule.years.reduce((a, b) => (astroHours(b) < astroHours(a) ? b : a));
const nAstro = rule.literals.filter((l) => l.id.includes('astro_geo_')).length;
---
<Base title="The rule card" description="One rule that fits all 32 presidential elections, and why it means nothing." current="rule">
  <h1>The rule card</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    One rule, built from astronomy and football, that gets every presidential election from 1900 to 2024 right.
    It does not know anything. This page shows the rule, every year of it, and exactly how it was picked.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="rule">{rule.sentence}</blockquote>

  <div class="scoreboard" aria-label="Scoreboard">
    <div><span class="n">{rule.correct_ec}/32</span><span class="l">elections matched (Electoral College coding)</span></div>
    <div><span class="n">{rule.popvote.correct}/32</span><span class="l">if &ldquo;win&rdquo; means the popular vote (wrong in {pvWrong})</span></div>
    <div><span class="n">{fmtPct(noise, 1)}</span><span class="l">of shuffled histories also get a perfect rule from the same search</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel warn">
    <h3>How this rule was chosen</h3>
    <p>
      The search found {f1.n_rules} perfect rules for &ldquo;the incumbent party wins&rdquo;. {sel.tied_top_key_rules} of them tie at the top of the
      frozen ranking. <strong>{sel.chosen_by} picked this one after seeing the results</strong>, because it contains no Moon
      condition and its tightest astronomy condition has the largest timing cushion in the tied set
      ({sel.featured_cushion_hours} hours of clock error, {fmtDeg(sel.featured_cushion_deg, 2)}). That cushion is shared by
      {sel.tied_sharing_best_cushion} of the {sel.tied_top_key_rules} tied rules, and {sel.tied_with_no_moon_literal} of the
      {sel.tied_top_key_rules} contain no Moon condition, so the choice among them was not forced. Picking one after the fact is
      one more way a search like this can flatter itself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the rule is made of</h2>
  <LiteralList literals={rule.literals} status={rule.verification_status} />
  <p class="sub">
    Astronomy conditions use the difference in ecliptic longitude (latitude is ignored) between two bodies at
    00:00 UTC on the stated date of the election year, as seen from the Earth&rsquo;s centre, with light-time, aberration and
    deflection applied and the true equinox of date. Dates are fixed in advance; every date is before Election Day.
  </p>

  <h2>All 32 elections</h2>
  <YearTable literals={rule.literals} years={rule.years} />
  <p class="sub">
    The row with the smallest cushion is highlighted: in {tightest.year}, a clock error of {astroHours(tightest).toFixed(1)}
    hours would flip its nearest astronomy condition.
  </p>

  <h2>If &ldquo;win&rdquo; means the popular vote</h2>
  <p>
    The same search, run on popular-vote coding (2000 and 2016 flip), finds {t.T1.popvote.n_minimal_perfect} rules for the
    incumbent party, {t.T2.popvote.n_minimal_perfect} for the Democratic Party and {t.T3.popvote.n_minimal_perfect} for the
    Republican Party. None of them is also perfect under Electoral College coding
    ({t.T1.rules_perfect_under_both_codings} rules in common for the incumbent party). This rule is wrong in {pvWrong} when
    scored that way.
  </p>

  <h2>It is not the only rule</h2>
  <p>
    {f1.n_rules} minimal perfect rules exist for this target ({f1.n_pareto} on the familiarity/length/timezone Pareto frontier).
    {f1.defunct_rules.count} of them contain a condition about a club that no longer exists
    (the Quebec Bulldogs, ranks {f1.defunct_rules.ranks.join(', ')}); they are flagged, still counted, and in the download.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>Show the {sel.tied_top_key_rules} rules tied at the top</summary>
    <TiedRules front={f1} featured={sel.featured_rank} />
  </details>

  <h2>How well was it checked?</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Astronomy ({v.horizons_comparisons} comparisons: {nAstro} conditions &times; {v.horizons_comparisons / nAstro} years):</strong> recomputed independently
        with NASA/JPL Horizons. The truth value differs in {v.horizons_truth_differs} years; the largest gap in the
        measured difference is {v.horizons_max_gap_deg.toFixed(5)}&deg; (tolerance {v.horizons_tolerance_deg}&deg;).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Super Bowl:</strong> date, winner and score agree with a second source (ESPN) for {v.espn_games_agree} of {v.espn_games_total}
        games; no game was played in the {noGameYears} election years before 1967.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Election outcomes:</strong> party of the sitting president, Electoral College winner and popular-vote winner agree with a
        second source ({v.outcomes_second_source}) in {v.outcomes_second_source_years_agree} of {v.outcomes_second_source_years} years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Human check: {rule.verification_status.human_checked} of {rule.verification_status.rows} human-checked, {rule.verification_status.automated_only} automated.</strong>
        {v.human_checks.length > 0 && <>
          The human-checked rows are the Pittsburgh Steelers&rsquo; Super Bowl wins in {v.human_checks.map((h) => h.year).join(' and ')}, confirmed by {v.human_checks[0].checked_by}
          against <a href={v.human_checks[0].source_url}>ESPN&rsquo;s list of winners</a> on {v.human_checks[0].checked_on} (those rows also passed the automated check). ESPN is also the source of the automated Super Bowl cross-check, so this is a person reading the same source, not a second independent one.
        </>}
        The automated checks above are not a substitute for checking the other {rule.verification_status.automated_only} rows by hand.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <div class="panel warn">
    <p>
      This is not a forecast and not evidence about elections. The search looked through {poolB} distinct candidate
      conditions, a pool deliberately made large, and the same procedure finds a perfect rule for
      shuffled histories {fmtPct(noise, 1)} of the time. The rule&rsquo;s conditions have no causal connection to voting.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
